# Revisions `git blame` should skip: mechanical, meaning-preserving reformats.
#
# GitHub honors this file automatically. Locally, once per clone:
#
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Only whole-tree formatting passes belong here, and only by their final SHA on
# `main` — an entry naming a commit that was rebased away is worse than no
# entry, because it silently ignores nothing while still claiming to. A commit
# that changes prose, code, or behavior must never be listed, however small.
#
# No entries yet, deliberately.
#
# The repo squash-merges, which means a pull request lands as one commit and
# the commits inside it stop existing. So a reformat only keeps its
# blame-ignore value if it lands as its **own pull request** — bundled with
# anything else, the squash fuses mechanical and real changes into a single
# SHA that this file may not list.
#
# That already happened once. The markdown reformat in #187 was a clean
# mechanical commit on the branch, but the squash (92596315) also carries
# `.prettierrc.json`, the prettier hook widening, `check-hook-filters.py`, and
# the semantic-line-breaks convention prose — roughly a hundred lines of real
# authorship. Listing it would blame-skip all of that, so it is not listed and
# that reformat is simply not ignorable. Next time: reformat alone, in its own
# PR, then add its SHA here.
